---
source: crates/qr-cli/tests/batch.rs
expression: help
---
Write one code per row of a CSV file (or per line of a text file) into a directory

Usage: qr batch [OPTIONS] --out-dir <DIR> <FILE>

Arguments:
  <FILE>
          CSV file to read, or with --lines a text file; '-' reads stdin

Options:
      --out-dir <DIR>
          Directory to write the codes in (created if it does not exist)

      --lines
          Read one item per line instead of CSV (blank lines are skipped)

      --no-header
          The CSV has no header: the first row is data even if a cell says 'data'

      --name <TEMPLATE>
          File name template, without the extension (see below)

  -f, --format <FORMAT>
          Output format [default: png]; text and ascii write .txt files

          Possible values:
          - text:  Unicode half blocks with no escape sequences
          - ascii: '##' and spaces, pure ASCII
          - png:   PNG image (1-bit indexed, or RGBA when transparent)
          - svg:   Scalable Vector Graphics
          - jpeg:  JPEG image (quality 92; at least 4 pixels per module)
          - webp:  Lossless WebP image
          - pdf:   One-page vector PDF
          - eps:   Encapsulated PostScript

      --ec <LEVEL>
          Error correction level: L, M, Q or H [default: M; H with --logo]

      --qr-version <N>
          Smallest symbol version to use, 1 to 40 (larger data still grows it)

      --mode <MODE>
          Data mode: auto, numeric, alnum or byte
          
          [default: auto]

      --mask <N>
          Force mask pattern 0 to 7 instead of picking one automatically

      --margin <N>
          Quiet zone around the code, in modules (below 4 may not scan)
          
          [default: 4]

      --scale <N>
          Size of one module in pixels (PNG, JPEG, WebP, SVG) or points (PDF, EPS) [default: 10]

      --size <PX>
          Width and height of a PNG, JPEG or WebP image in pixels, with the largest whole module
          size that fits (vector formats are at most this size)

      --dpi <N>
          Pixel density to record in a PNG or JPEG file, in dots per inch

      --fg <COLOR>
          Colour of dark modules: #RGB, #RRGGBB, #RRGGBBAA or a CSS name [default: black]

      --bg <COLOR>
          Colour of light modules and the quiet zone [default: white]

      --transparent
          Leave the background unpainted (image formats only)

      --invert
          Swap dark and light modules (or --fg and --bg for images)

      --shape <SHAPE>
          How dark modules are drawn in png, jpeg, webp, svg, pdf and eps output; the three finder
          patterns stay square

          Possible values:
          - square:  Full squares: the standard look
          - rounded: Squares with their exposed corners rounded
          - dots:    Circles
          
          [default: square]

      --logo <FILE>
          Draw a PNG, JPEG or WebP image (at most 10 MiB and 8192x8192) over the centre of a png,
          jpeg, webp or svg code. Sets --ec H (a lower --ec is overridden with a warning) and checks
          the code reads back

      --logo-scale <FRACTION>
          Width of the --logo as a fraction of the code's width, 0.05 to 0.3 [default: 0.2]

      --verify
          Read the rendered code back with a decoder and fail (exit 6) unless it holds the input.
          Automatic for images with a --logo or a non-default --shape/--fg/--bg, --transparent or
          --invert. Text output is checked as a plain module grid (the symbol, not the characters)

      --no-verify
          Skip the automatic read-back check

  -j, --jobs <N>
          Worker threads [default: the number of CPUs]

      --fail-fast
          Stop at the first row that fails (rows already started finish)

      --force
          Replace files that already exist in --out-dir

      --json
          Print one JSON object per row and then a summary on stdout (NDJSON), and JSON errors on
          stderr

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use colour; NO_COLOR and CLICOLOR_FORCE are honoured in auto

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Colour when writing to a terminal, unless the environment says not to
          - always: Always colour
          - never:  Never colour
          
          [default: auto]

  -q, --quiet
          Print errors only, no warnings

  -v, --verbose
          Also print details on stderr (what was encoded, or decoded, and how)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Input: a CSV file whose first row is a header when one of its cells is
'data' (that column is encoded; a 'filename' column names the files), or
without a header, data in the first column and a file name in the second.
With --lines, every non-blank line is one code. At most 50 MiB, 100000
rows and 1000 cells per row; a UTF-8 byte order mark is ignored.

File names: --name is a template [default: {filename} when there is a
filename column, otherwise the row number] with the fields {n:04} (the
row number padded to 4 digits; 'n' alone in braces does not pad it),
{hash} (16 hex digits of the data), {data}, {filename} and, with a
header, any column by name. {data} puts the data itself in the name, so
avoid it for secrets such as WiFi passwords or URL tokens. Field values
lose path separators, ':', control characters and leading '.' or '-';
Windows device names (CON, NUL, COM1, ...) are refused; names are cut at
200 bytes, must be unique ignoring case and Unicode normalization, and
get the format's extension. Every file stays directly inside --out-dir.

Existing files are never replaced without --force. A row that fails is
reported and the rest are still written, unless --fail-fast; the exit
status is then that of the first failed row. Each file is written
atomically but not flushed to disk one by one. Styled codes (--logo,
--shape, colours) are read back automatically, about a fifth of the
batch time; --no-verify skips that for large runs.

Examples:
  qr batch urls.csv --out-dir codes
                               One PNG per row, named from the CSV
  qr batch urls.txt --lines --out-dir codes --name 'url-{n:04}' -f svg
                               One SVG per line: url-0001.svg, ...
  qr batch rows.csv --out-dir out --json > report.ndjson
                               One JSON object per row, then a summary
